### Rule details

This rule warns when `String.prototype.replace` or `String.prototype.replaceAll` is called with **replacement strings containing special `$` substitution tokens** (`$&`, ``$` ``, `$'`, `$1`–`$99`, etc.) in contexts where they are likely unintended or unsafe — especially when the search or replacement arguments are non-literal. Developers frequently assume that `.replaceAll("text", "replacement")` is a literal substitution. However, **all** of JavaScript’s `$` replacement tokens still apply, even when the search argument is a plain string.

### Related ECMAScript feature

Not new — this is long-standing but confusing behavior defined in https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-getsubstitution

### What type of rule is this?

Warns about a potential problem

### Example code

```js
// ❌ Problematic

// `$&` expands to the match, even with string search
"foo BAR baz".replaceAll("BAR", "($&)");
// → "foo (BAR) baz"

// `$'` inserts suffix text after the match (unexpected duplication)
"foo BAR baz".replace("BAR", "($')");
// → "foo ( baz) baz"

// `$`` inserts prefix text before the match (unexpected duplication)
"foo BAR baz".replace("BAR", "($`)")
// → "foo (foo ) baz"

// `$1` expands only when a regex capture exists
"price template PLACEHOLDER x".replace(/PLACEHOLDER (.+)/, "$1 bucks");
// → "price template x bucks"

// Non-literal arguments make `$` behavior unpredictable
text.replaceAll(searchVar, replacementVar);

// ✅ Safe

// Literal replacement — no tokens
"foo BAR baz".replaceAll("BAR", "(BAR)");

// Function replacer — disables all `$` substitutions
"foo BAR baz".replaceAll("BAR", () => "(BAR)");

// Intentional literal `$`
"price template PLACEHOLDER".replace("PLACEHOLDER", "$$10 replacement $$&");
// → "price template $10 replacement $&"

// `$0` and `$n` with no capture remain literal
"PLACEHOLDER".replace(/PLACEHOLDER/, "$0 test"); // " $0 test"
"PLACEHOLDER".replace(/PLACEHOLDER/, "$2 test"); // " $2 test"
```

### Why should this rule be in the core instead of a plugin?

* This is a **core JavaScript language pitfall**, and it keeps staying very underdiscussed.
* It can cause real-world logic and security bugs (e.g. user data containing `$&` unexpectedly expands).
* A core rule helps developers reason about safe literal replacement, function replacers, or explicit escaping.

### **Rationale: Variable arguments**

If either argument is a variable, `$` expansion safety can’t be determined statically:

* The **search variable** might hold a RegExp with capture groups (activating `$1`–`$99`).
* The **replacement variable** might contain `$&`, `$'`, or ``$` `` from user input.
* Only the **function replacer** guarantees literal, predictable behavior.

---

### **Clarification on `$` token semantics (verified by Node tests)**

| Token      | Meaning                | Always active?      | Example (string search)                                   |
| ---------- | ---------------------- | ------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |
| `$$`       | literal `$`            | ✅                   | `"foo BAR".replace("BAR", "$$x") → "foo $x"`              |
| `$&`       | entire match           | ✅                   | `"foo BAR".replace("BAR", "($&)") → "foo (BAR)"`          |
| `"$TICK"`    | prefix before match    | ✅                   | `"foo BAR".replace("BAR", "($TICK)") → "foo (foo ) baz"` (I'm using TICK here for \` symbol because it breaks GH rendering)     |
| `$'`       | suffix after match     | ✅                   | `"foo BAR baz".replace("BAR", "($')") → "foo ( baz) baz"` |
| `$1`–`$99` | capture groups         | ✅ when group exists | `/PLACEHOLDER (.+)/` → `"price template x bucks"`         |
| `$0`       | literal (no expansion) | ✅ safe              | `"BAR".replace("BAR", "$0") → "$0"`                       |

---

### **References**

* [Tweet showing real-world confusion](https://x.com/JLarky/status/1984489642184622542)
* [Archived related issue](https://github.com/eslint/eslint/issues/13521)

